    {
      "id": 14,
      "type": "graph",
      "title": "Judge position bias",
      "description": "How far a tournament's judge preferred whichever hypothesis it happened to be shown first, 0.0 being no order effect and 1.0 the order deciding every pair. It is the only in-run proxy for judge quality this feature has, and the ranking is worth nothing without it: simulated against a constructed ordering, top-1 recovery is under 40% at a 75%-accurate judge (`make hypothesis-recovery`). A gateway model swap that made the judge prefer the first side would turn the feature into noise while every other signal stayed green \u2014 the ratings would still have intervals, the jobs would still complete, and the table a chemist reads would still look ordered. Suppressed below eight decisive comparisons, so a flat line with tournaments completing means the fields are too small to measure rather than unbiased.",
      "datasource": "prometheus",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 36
      },
      "span": 6,
      "fill": 0,
